## What the experiment says as it runs: its steps, board and answer. These texts come with the experiment when it opens; its card is in strobe-photo.ftl.

strobe-photo-labstrobe-say = The first photo: the ball, down from the groove, rolls along the table. Put a ruler to the photo and measure the gaps between the ball’s images.
strobe-photo-labstrobe-button = 📷 Photo: the ball on the table
strobe-photo-labstrobe-plan = the photo of the ball on the table: the gaps
strobe-photo-labstrobe-say-2 = The second photo: the ball rolls down the groove from rest. Measure the gaps between the images here too.
strobe-photo-labstrobe-button-2 = 📷 Photo: the ball in the groove
strobe-photo-labstrobe-plan-2 = the photo of the ball in the groove: the gaps
strobe-photo-answer-why = The stroboscope flashes at equal intervals — here every 0.25 s — and the photo keeps where the ball was at each of those instants. So the gap between neighbouring images is the path in 0.25 s. The photo is 5 times smaller: what the ruler gives times 5 is the real distance; divided by 0.25 s — the speed. On the table the gaps are equal: the ball moved uniformly. In the groove they grow — about as 1 : 3 : 5 : 7 — the ball was speeding up, its average speed on each gap greater than on the one before. Distances on maps and plans are measured the same way: a scale of 1 : 100 000 means 1 cm on the map is 1 km on the ground.
strobe-photo-answer-say = On the table the gaps are equal — { $table } mm on the photo each, { $table2 } in life in 0.25 s: { $table3 }.
